y own experience or the  experience of people that i see learning  around me but i like reading so i like  reading about other people's experiences  so i've got books here on toefl and  toeic and language teaching methodology  and what we got here vocabulary phrases  and idioms essential words for toefl  vocabulary language seeking psychology  for language teachers learning  vocabulary  uh how languages are learned the second  language acquisition by ella's which is  a bit of a  of a well-known work assessing  vocabulary and on and on  you know things like identity and  language learning i bought that just to  read just how bad it was going to be you  know full of all the sort of power  relationships and stuff language  teaching and i can tell you  that everything here  and in three times this pile is  essentially  useless  uh lots of obtuse references to studies  and i can't even remember you know  either  proving the obvious or trying trying to  prove something that isn't very obvious  and in these two little books  by crash foreign language education the  easy way  which i picked up somewhere  in taiwan i guess it was when i was in  taiwan stephen krashen was speaking  there and this  whoops explorations and language  acquisition and use the taipei lectures  and  this man is brilliant  there is no question and i heartily  heartily subscribe to most of what he  says we have some things that link that  we do differently and i'll get to that  but he has these five principles of  language acquisition and these i think  are very very important language  acquisition is  a subconscious process while it is  happening we are not aware that it is  happening we do not learn consciously we  we work on some things now and they  don't  seem to reappear in our brain appear in  our brain film much later we stop  learning we start up again we're better  there's a whole  subconscious fermentation going on in  the brain that we are not aware of very  important point second  hypothesis the natural order hypothesis  the natural order hypothesis claims that  we acquired the parts of a language in a  predictable order  and it says there are three amazing  facts about the natural order  the natural order is not based on any  features of simplicity and complexity  that is absolutely true some structures  you learned earlier like  the continuous form or i am going to go  i will be going all of those things are  easier to learn than trying to remember  the third person singular  it is it's true in spanish third person  singular i can't remember it half the  time i far rather say  jo boy  because then you have to try to remember  first person it's more difficult  so  it's not based on the obvious features  of simplicity and complexity two  the natural order cannot be changed it  is immune to deliberate teaching  teachers out there immune  and the third point is  that the is that that he says one might  suppose that the solution to our problem  is simply to teach along the natural  order  we need only to find out which items are  naturally acquired early and teach those  first  but the third amazing fact is that it is  not the solution the natural order is  not the teaching order he gets into that  later on the next is what he calls the  monitor hypothesis which means that if  you learn grammar you develop a monitor  whereby you check what you're about to  say and he says while the monitor can  make a small contribution to accuracy  the research indicates that acquisition  makes the major contribution so your  accuracy in the language will come from  your input  activities  acquisition is responsible for both  fluency and most of our accuracy  because  to use the monitor in other words  grammar you must a know the rule which  is difficult you have to remember the  rule and you have to focus on form when  you are trying to focus on meaning which  is very difficult to do  and typically you don't have the time  then the next thing he goes on to is the  in